be— —unconscious, ideal, and remote. One day, a fatal day I sometimes

206
00:17:28,439 --> 00:17:32,799
think, I determined to paint a wonderful portrait of you as you actually are, not

207
00:17:32,799 --> 00:17:38,879
in the costume of dead ages, but in your own dress and in your own time. Whether

208
00:17:38,879 --> 00:17:43,760
it was the realism of the method, or the mere wonder of your own personality, thus

209
00:17:43,760 --> 00:17:48,959
directly presented to me without mist or veil, I cannot tell. But I know that as I

210
00:17:48,959 --> 00:17:53,799
worked at it, every flake and film of color seemed to me to reveal my

211
00:17:53,799 --> 00:18:01,158
secret. I grew afraid that others would know of my idolatry. I felt, Dorian, that I had told

212
00:18:01,158 --> 00:18:06,959
too much, that I'd put too much of myself into it. Then it was that I resolved

213
00:18:06,959 --> 00:18:11,478
never to allow the picture to be exhibited. You were a little annoyed; but

214
00:18:11,478 --> 00:18:15,799
then you did not realize all that it meant to me. Harry, to whom I talked about

215
00:18:15,799 --> 00:18:19,719
it, laughed at me. But I did not mind that.

216
00:18:19,719 --> 00:18:25,199
When the picture was finished, and I sat alone with it, I felt that I was

217
00:18:25,199 --> 00:18:29,920
right.... Well, after a few days the thing left my studio, and as soon as I got rid

218
00:18:29,920 --> 00:18:34,279
of the intolerable fascination of its presence, it seemed to me that I'd been

219
00:18:34,279 --> 00:18:38,079
foolish in imagining that I'd seen anything in it, more than that you were

220
00:18:38,079 --> 00:18:43,398
extremely good-looking and that I could paint. Even now I cannot help feeling

221
00:18:43,398 --> 00:18:47,359
that it is a mistake to think that the passion one feels in creation is ever

222
00:18:47,359 --> 00:18:52,158
really shown in the work one creates. Art is always more abstract than

223
00:18:52,158 --> 00:18:56,760
we fancy. Form and color tell us of form and color that is

224
00:18:56,760 --> 00:19:01,719
all. It often seems to me that art conceals the artist far more completely

225
00:19:01,719 --> 00:19:07,040
than it ever reveals him. And so when I got this offer from Paris, I determined

226
00:19:07,040 --> 00:19:12,559
to make your portrait the principal thing in my exhibition. It never occurred to me that

227
00:19:12,559 --> 00:19:17,040
you would refuse. I see now that you were right. The

228
00:19:17,040 --> 00:19:21,600
picture cannot be shown. You must not be angry with me, Dorian, for what I have

229
00:19:21,600 --> 00:19:25,879
told you. As I said to Harry, once, you are made to be

230
00:19:25,879 --> 00:19:29,920
worshiped Dorian Gray drew a long breath.

231
00:19:29,920 --> 00:19:35,520
The color came back to to his cheeks, and a smile played about his lips. The peril

232
00:19:35,520 --> 00:19:41,559
was over. He was safe for the time. Yet he could not help feeling infinite pity for

233
00:19:41,559 --> 00:19:45,799
the painter who had just made this strange confession to him, and wondered

234
00:19:45,799 --> 00:19:50,879
if he himself would ever be so dominated by the personality of a friend. Lord

235
00:19:50,879 --> 00:19:55,920
Henry had the charm of being very dangerous. But that was all. He was too

236
00:19:55,920 --> 00:20:01,639
clever and too cynical to be really fond of. Would there never be someone who

237
00:20:01,639 --> 00:20:04,359
would fill him with a strange idolatry?

238
00:20:04,359 --> 00:20:08,879
Was that one of the things that life had in store? “It is extraordinary to me,

239
00:20:08,879 --> 00:20:13,719
Dorian,” said holwood that you should have seen this in the portrait. Did you really

240
00:20:13,719 --> 00:20:19,478
see it?” “I saw something in it,” he answered, “something that seemed to me very

241
00:20:19,478 --> 00:20:25,760
curious.” “Well, you don’t mind my looking at the thing now?” Dorian shook his head.

242
00:20:25,760 --> 00:20:31,760
“You must not ask me that, Basil. I could not possibly let you stand in front of that

243
00:20:31,760 --> 00:20:41,199
picture.” “You will someday surely?” “Never.” “Well, perhaps you are right. And now goodbye

244
00:20:41,199 --> 00:20:46,158
Dorian. You have been the one person in my life who has really influenced my art.

245
00:20:46,158 --> 00:20:48,158
Whatever I have done that is good, I owe

246
00:20:48,158 --> 00:20:51,600
to you. Ah! you don’t know what it cost me

247
00:20:51,600 --> 00:20:57,279
to tell you all that I've told you.” “My dear Basil,” said Dorian, “what have you

248
00:20:57,279 --> 00:21:02,279
told me? Simply that you felt that you admired me too much. That is not even a

249
00:21:02,279 --> 00:21:09,238
compliment.” “It was not intended as a compliment. It was a confession. Now that I've made it,

250
00:21:09,238 --> 00:21:11,639
something seems to have gone out of me.

251
00:21:11,639 --> 00:21:17,439
Perhaps one should never put one’s worship into words.” “It was a very disappointing

252
00:21:17,439 --> 00:21:21,559
confession.” “Why, what did you expect, Dorian? You didn’t see anything else in

253
00:21:21,559 --> 00:21:27,238
the picture, did you? There was nothing else to see?” “No; there was nothing else to

254
00:21:27,238 --> 00:21:33,719
see. Why do you ask? But you mustn’t talk about worship. It is foolish. You and I

255
00:21:33,719 --> 00:21:40,719
are friends, Basil, and we must always remain so.” “You have got Harry,” said the painter

256
00:21:40,719 --> 00:21:46,199
sadly. “Oh, Harry!” cried the lad, with a ripple of laughter. “Harry spends his days

257
00:21:46,199 --> 00:21:50,279
in saying what is incredible and his evenings in doing what is

258
00:21:50,279 --> 00:21:55,639
improbable. Just the sort of life I would like to lead. But still I don’t think I

259
00:21:55,639 --> 00:22:01,680
would go to Harry if I were in trouble. I would sooner go to you, Basil.”

260
00:22:01,680 --> 00:22:07,680
“You will sit to me again?” “Impossible!” “You spoil my life as an artist by refusing,

261
00:22:07,680 --> 00:22:13,680
Dorian. No man comes across two ideal things. Few come across one.” “I can’t

262
00:22:13,680 --> 00:22:18,318
explain it to you, Basil, but I must never sit to you again. There is something

263
00:22:18,318 --> 00:22:23,000
fatal about a portrait. It has a life of its own. I will come and have tea with

264
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:29,639
you. That will be just as pleasant.” “Pleasanter for you I'm afraid,” murmured hallwood

265
00:22:29,639 --> 00:22:33,920
regretfully. “And now goodbye I'm sorry you won’t let me look

266
00:22:33,920 --> 00:22:39,238
at the picture once again. But that can’t be helped. I quite understand what you

267
00:22:39,238 --> 00:22:48,000
feel about it.” As he left the room, Dorian Gray smiled to himself. Poor basle How

268
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:52,559
little he knew of the true reason! And how strange it was that, instead of

269
00:22:52,559 --> 00:22:58,600
having been forced to reveal his own secret, he had succeeded, almost by chance,

270
00:22:58,600 --> 00:23:01,439
in resting a secret from his friend!

271
00:23:01,439 --> 00:23:05,680
How much that strange confession explained to him! The painter’s absurd

272
00:23:05,680 --> 00:23:12,040
fits of jealousy, his wild devotion, his extravagant panics his curious

273
00:23:12,040 --> 00:23:18,079
reticences— —he understood them all now, and he felt sorry. There seemed to him to be

274
00:23:18,079 --> 00:23:23,079
something tragic in a friendship so colored by romance. He sighed and touched

275
00:23:23,079 --> 00:23:28,478
the bell. The portrait must be hidden away at all costs. He could not run such

276
00:23:28,478 --> 00:23:33,520
a risk of discovery again. It had been mad of him to have allowed the thing to

277
00:23:33,520 --> 00:23:39,000
remain, even for an hour, in a room to which any of his friends had
